    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_e3d35a5cd3e120fa459fe10d82cb3e58cac9a68627feec3dc639f7f979e4727f",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Both counterexample computations replace blind search over enormous x by a zero-based oscillatory representation plus finite error control, but they realize the search differently: the pi-minus-Li case uses a certified zero table with Lehman bounds to prove reversal on an interval, whereas the Mertens sketch chooses phases by simultaneous Diophantine approximation and LLL to force a large normalized sum.",
